<h2>Should You Paint Over Wallpaper or Remove It First?</h2><p>This is the first question every homeowner asks, and the honest answer is: it depends on your walls, your wallpaper, and how long you plan to stay in the house. Painting directly over wallpaper can save a weekend of messy work, but it can also create problems that cost far more to fix later. Understanding the tradeoffs before you commit is the most important step you can take.</p><p>Removal is almost always the better long-term choice. It gives you a clean, stable surface and eliminates the risk of seams showing through your finished coat. That said, removal is not always practical. In older homes throughout Springfield, Holyoke, and Westfield, wallpaper was sometimes applied directly to unfinished drywall or plaster. In those cases, pulling the paper off can damage the wall so severely that you end up doing a full skim-coat repair anyway. If that is your situation, painting over may actually be the smarter call.</p><h3>When Painting Over Wallpaper Makes Sense</h3><p>Painting over is a reasonable option when the wallpaper is fully adhered with no bubbles or peeling edges, when there is only one layer, and when the surface is smooth enough that seams will not read through the paint. If you press on the wall and hear a hollow crinkle, or if edges are lifting near baseboards or corners, those are red flags. Any area that is not bonded will telegraph right through your paint job within months.</p><h3>When You Should Always Remove First</h3><p>Multiple layers of wallpaper, vinyl-coated paper, or any sign of moisture damage behind the wall are all situations where removal is non-negotiable. Water-damaged walls in Western Massachusetts are more common than people expect, particularly in older Agawam and West Springfield homes that went through years of harsh New England winters with imperfect insulation. Painting over hidden moisture damage traps the problem and can lead to mold growth behind the wall.</p><h2>Preparing Wallpaper for Paint: The Steps That Actually Matter</h2><figure class="blog-figure"><img src="/images/stock/web/blog-how-to-paint-over-wallpaper-what-western-mass-homeowners-should-know-2.jpg" alt="Cut-in work along trim during an interior repaint" width="1100" height="619" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure><p>If you have decided to paint over, preparation is everything. Skipping steps here is what leads to the bubbling, peeling, and visible seams that give painted-over wallpaper its bad reputation.</p><p>Start by walking every inch of the wall and pressing lightly to find any sections that have lifted or bubbled. Use a seam repair adhesive to glue those sections back down firmly, then press them flat and let them dry completely. Next, skim over every seam with joint compound, feathering it out several inches on each side. Sand smooth once dry. This step alone makes the difference between a professional-looking result and one that looks like a shortcut.</p><p>After repairs, apply a oil-based primer or a shellac-based primer to the entire surface. This is not optional. Water-based primers can reactivate the old wallpaper paste underneath and cause the paper to bubble and lift. The oil-based coat seals everything in place and gives your topcoat something stable to bond to. <a href="https://www.sherwin-williams.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Sherwin-Williams</a> recommends this approach specifically to prevent moisture from penetrating the paper before the topcoat is applied.</p><h3>Dealing With Textured or Embossed Wallpaper</h3><p>Heavily textured or embossed wallpaper presents an extra challenge. Even with careful priming, the pattern can ghost through the finished surface, especially under raking light. If the texture is prominent, you have two options: apply a skim coat of joint compound over the entire surface to flatten it out, or reconsider removal. A thin skim coat adds labor but can produce a wall that looks like new drywall once it is sanded and primed.</p><div class="blog-cta-inline"><p>Would you rather we handled it? Estimates are always free.</p><a class="btn btn--gold" href="/contact.html#estimate-form">Get a Free Estimate</a></div><h2>Choosing the Right Paint for the Job</h2><p>Once your primer is dry, you want a high-quality interior latex paint in a finish that makes sense for the room. Eggshell or satin finishes are good choices for living rooms and bedrooms because they are easier to clean than flat paint while still hiding minor surface imperfections better than a semi-gloss. Avoid flat paint over wallpaper entirely. Flat finishes make future cleaning harder and tend to show any texture variation more than a satin will.</p><p>Apply at least two coats, allowing full dry time between coats. Rushing this step by painting the second coat while the first is still soft is one of the most common DIY mistakes. In humid New England summers, drying times can run longer than the label suggests, so give each coat plenty of time before you evaluate coverage.</p><p>Color choice also matters more than people realize when painting over wallpaper. Very dark or very saturated colors may require three coats to cover a patterned paper completely, particularly if the original design had strong contrast. If you are painting a light color over a dark geometric pattern, a tinted primer that matches your topcoat can cut down on the number of finish coats you need.</p><h2>Lead Paint and Older Homes: A Western Mass Safety Note</h2><p>If your home was built before 1978, there is a real chance that layers of lead-based paint exist somewhere in that wall assembly, potentially under or above the wallpaper. For homes built before 1978, <a href="https://www.epa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">the EPA</a> establishes the rules for disturbing lead paint, including requirements around testing, containment, and cleanup. This applies to contractors and homeowners alike. Before you start cutting, sanding, or scraping anything in an older Springfield, Holyoke, or Westfield home, it is worth picking up a lead test kit from a hardware store or having a certified inspector assess the walls.</p><p>This is not a reason to panic, but it is a reason to be informed. Working carefully, keeping the area ventilated, and wearing appropriate protective gear are basic steps that protect everyone in the household.</p><h2>Knowing When to Call a Professional</h2><p>Some wallpaper projects are genuinely well-suited to a confident DIYer with a free weekend. Others are not. If you are dealing with multiple layers, damaged plaster, moisture concerns, or a large open-plan area where every imperfection will be visible, the case for bringing in a professional grows quickly. <a href="/services.html#interior-painting">our interior painting service</a> can help you assess whether your specific walls are good candidates for painting over or whether removal and prep work will serve you better in the long run.</p><p>Our team regularly works with homeowners across Western Massachusetts who started a wallpaper project themselves and ran into complications they were not expecting. There is no shame in that. Wallpaper removal and surface prep are genuinely skilled work, and getting the base right is what determines whether your painted walls look great for ten years or start showing problems in the first spring thaw.</p><section class="blog-faq"><h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Can you paint directly over wallpaper without priming first?</h3><p>No, priming is an essential step and should not be skipped. Applying water-based paint directly to wallpaper can reactivate the old adhesive underneath and cause bubbling or lifting. An oil-based or shellac-based primer seals the surface and gives your topcoat a stable base to bond to.</p><h3>How do you keep wallpaper seams from showing through paint?</h3><p>Fill every seam with joint compound and feather it out several inches on each side before priming. Sand smooth once dry, then apply your primer coat. If seams are still visible after priming, apply a second thin layer of compound, sand again, and re-prime before applying your finish coat.</p><h3>Is it better to remove wallpaper or paint over it?</h3><p>Removal is the better long-term choice in most situations because it gives you a clean surface without hidden risk. However, if the wallpaper is fully bonded and removing it would damage the drywall or plaster underneath, painting over with proper prep can be a practical alternative.</p><h3>How many coats of paint do you need over wallpaper?</h3><p>Plan on a minimum of two finish coats after your primer. If you are covering a strongly patterned or dark wallpaper with a lighter color, you may need three coats for even coverage. Using a tinted primer that is close to your topcoat color can reduce the number of finish coats required.</p><h3>Does painting over wallpaper cause mold?</h3><p>It can, if moisture is already present in the wall or if the primer and paint trap humidity behind the paper over time. This is a particular concern in older New England homes. If you notice any staining, soft spots, or musty smell before you paint, investigate the source of moisture before proceeding.</p></section>
